WEBVTT

00:00:02.484 --> 00:00:04.514
Okay, so let me show you something.

00:00:04.564 --> 00:00:05.794
You know flashcards?

00:00:05.844 --> 00:00:08.104
Question on the front, answer on the back.

00:00:08.124 --> 00:00:10.624
You quiz yourself, and the app brings each card

00:00:10.644 --> 00:00:12.314
back right before you'd forget it.

00:00:12.973 --> 00:00:14.393
You tell it how well you knew it,

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:16.580
and that decides when you see it again.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.803
And honestly, that part works great.

00:00:19.846 --> 00:00:22.476
The catch is, a card is pretty much just

00:00:22.726 --> 00:00:23.266
text.

00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:25.596
And so much of what we actually learn isn't

00:00:25.846 --> 00:00:26.306
text.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:26.946
So...

00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:29.036
what if a card could be anything?

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.990
Like, learning chess?

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.150
Find the checkmate, right there on the board.

00:00:33.474 --> 00:00:34.174
Piano?

00:00:34.194 --> 00:00:35.784
Play the chord it asks for.

00:00:36.377 --> 00:00:37.287
Japanese?

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.917
Draw the character with your finger,

00:00:38.937 --> 00:00:41.287
and the AI tells you how your strokes look.

00:00:41.694 --> 00:00:42.954
Got a coding interview?

00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:45.194
Solve the problem, and the tests run the second

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:46.620
you hit check.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.420
Chemistry?

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.942
Balance the equation.

00:00:48.962 --> 00:00:51.142
Math gives you new numbers every time,

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:52.382
so you learn the method,

00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:53.772
not the answer.

00:00:53.792 --> 00:00:54.572
History?

00:00:54.592 --> 00:00:57.185
Put the events in order on a timeline.

00:00:57.205 --> 00:00:58.105
Ear training?

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:00.225
Listen to two notes and say how far apart

00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:01.641
they are.

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:02.601
Even trivia:

00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:04.241
how many times does your heart beat in a

00:01:04.261 --> 00:01:05.211
lifetime?

00:01:16.001 --> 00:01:18.141
And all of it runs on FSRS,

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:20.341
the same scheduler modern Anki uses,

00:01:20.361 --> 00:01:22.461
so every card comes back right when you need

00:01:22.481 --> 00:01:22.951
it.

00:01:23.461 --> 00:01:25.604
And it's built to work really well with AI

00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:26.457
agents.

00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:28.524
You don't need one, most cards are checked by

00:01:28.544 --> 00:01:29.614
little scripts right on your

00:01:29.904 --> 00:01:30.404
computer.

00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:31.497
But if you have one,

00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:33.444
it can do everything you can:

00:01:33.464 --> 00:01:35.324
turn your lecture notes into cards,

00:01:35.344 --> 00:01:38.084
grade your explanations, read your stats and tell you

00:01:38.104 --> 00:01:39.524
what to review next.

00:01:39.544 --> 00:01:42.684
Sign in with ChatGPT, bring your own API key,

00:01:42.704 --> 00:01:44.364
or just let your agent drive it from the

00:01:44.384 --> 00:01:45.214
command line.

00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:48.677
And this is my favorite part.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.147
Every card is a little program you can open

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.267
and change.

00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:53.707
Or just ask your agent:

00:01:53.727 --> 00:01:55.840
make me a card that teaches Morse code.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.797
A minute later, it's in your deck.

00:01:57.847 --> 00:01:59.957
Oh, and you can change the themes too.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.580
It's called Etude.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.370
It's free, it's open source,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.490
and it's on GitHub.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:06.380
Go try it.
